# Commit Messages

Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) with an
imperative subject. This is project convention, not a CI gate.

## Format

```text
<type>(optional-scope): <imperative summary>

Optional body: why it matters, constraints, or follow-ups.
```

## Types

| Type | Use for |
| --- | --- |
| `feat` | New user-facing or API capability |
| `fix` | Bug fix or incorrect behavior |
| `refactor` | Structure/cleanup with no intended behavior change |
| `docs` | Documentation only |
| `test` | Tests only |
| `perf` | Performance improvement |
| `build` | Docker, Compose, packaging, or CI wiring |
| `chore` | Tooling, deps, config, hygiene, or misc non-product work |

## Rules

- Imperative mood in the subject (`add`, `fix`, `harden` — not `added` / `fixes`).
- Subject ~72 characters or less; no trailing period.
- Scope is optional, lowercase, and short (`api`, `gui`, `auth`, `docker`, `mcp`). Omit when unclear.
- Prefer one logical change per commit when practical.
- Subject states the outcome; put file laundry lists and long detail in the body.
- Never commit secrets, credentials, tokens, or private personal data.
- Create commits only when the user explicitly asks.
